I'm running Nginx 1.24.0 on my Ubuntu 22.04.3 LTS server. It's a reliable and high-performance web server that I've configured to serve multiple domains from a single instance. I've got it loaded with the `nginx-full` package from the Ubuntu repository, and I've made sure to keep it up to date with the latest security patches.

By default, Nginx is listening on port 80 (HTTP) and port 443 (HTTPS), with a self-signed SSL/TLS certificate generated using OpenSSL. My `nginx.conf` file is located in the `/etc/nginx` directory, and I've made some customizations to the configuration to optimize performance and security.

Here's a snippet of my `nginx.conf` file:
```bash
http {
    ...
    server {
        listen 80;
        server_name example.com;
        root /var/www/example.com;
        index index.html;
        ...
    }
    server {
        listen 443 ssl;
        server_name example.com;
        ssl_certificate /etc/nginx/ssl/example.com.crt;
        ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/ssl/example.com.key;
        ...
    }
}
```
